OWNER RULING, 2026-08-15, on whether an overlay may declare a module
enumerated: "i mean what else would it do? you have a better idea? if not,
then of course it may."

WHAT WAS BROKEN. An overlay could declare what a third-party module DOES
(primitive rows) but never that the list was COMPLETE -- load_overlay_catalog
refused stdlib_module_completeness outright. So a dependency could never leave
the uncatalogued-module set no matter how carefully it was described, and
verify-claims could never answer `confirmed` for any repo that HAS
dependencies. Measured on hypergumbo's own self-survey: 52 genuine third-party
modules, permanently unadjudicable by construction.

THE RENAME IS THE OTHER HALF, and it is the owner's point rather than
housekeeping: "overlays are where third-party stuff goes. am i wrong? then why
would we put anything about stdlib in its associated names? that would be
misleading."

  module_completeness           canonical, accepted everywhere
  stdlib_module_completeness    deprecated; still READ from shipped
                                catalogues so no curated file breaks;
                                REFUSED in an overlay, with a message
                                naming the honest key rather than a flat
                                rejection

The concept was never stdlib-specific -- only its AUTHORS were, which is a fact
about who may write the key, not about what it means. Once an overlay may
declare numpy or tree_sitter enumerated, a field named stdlib_* holds two
populations under a name describing one: the conceptual leak the
fundamental-concept audit exists to catch.

WHAT BOUNDS THE GRANT, since it is the single grant of confirmability
(INV-buzab) and therefore the most powerful line a user can write here:
  - `retrieved:` stays MANDATORY, so an entry is a dated audit record rather
    than a switch;
  - it still does not promote the module into stdlib_modules, so vouching for
    a dependency's I/O never restates itself as provenance (ADR-0041 section 3).

THE COST IS KEPT ON THE RECORD RATHER THAN SOFTENED. A six-line overlay with
zero rows and one telnetlib entry turns the INV-buzab exfiltration fixture --
which opens telnetlib.Telnet and writes os.environ["API_KEY"] into it -- from
inconclusive rc 2 to confirmed rc 0. The test that used to pin the REFUSAL now
pins the GRANT, deliberately still spelled with telnetlib: if anyone narrows
this later, the test they must change is the one carrying the exfiltration
fixture in its docstring.

A TEST THAT WOULD HAVE PASSED EITHER WAY, caught and fixed: the first version
of the refusal-spelling test used match="module_completeness", which is a
SUBSTRING of "stdlib_module_completeness" and therefore matched the old message
too. It now asserts the message actively steers the author.

KNOWN RESIDUAL, filed as INV-tabaf rather than fixed here: the stderr
disclosure names the overlay PATH but not which modules it vouched for, so the
most powerful line in a user's overlay is the least visible one. It needs the
disclosure to move after the per-language catalogue loop, which is a
restructure and does not belong in a rename-plus-permission commit.

ADR-0016 amended in place (the refusal paragraph rewritten, not banner-flagged).

